#!/bin/sh
# ============================================================
# acpid service — run script
#
# `novi-power suspend` has existed since RFC 0011 and nothing has ever
# called it. Closing a laptop lid did nothing; pressing the power
# button did nothing. Both are worse than they sound: a machine that
# does not sleep when the lid shuts goes into a bag at 80% and comes
# out flat, having run at full tilt in an enclosed space, and a power
# button that does nothing teaches people to hold it for four seconds,
# which is a hard cut with filesystems mounted.
#
# busybox acpid reads every /dev/input/event* when given no -e, maps
# EV_KEY/KEY_POWER and EV_SW/SW_LID against a table compiled into it,
# and runs $confdir/<action>. The two handlers in /etc/acpi/ are stubs
# whose paths that table dictates; the policy they reach is declared
# state (power.lid, power.button), read at event time.
#
# -f: foreground, so s6 supervises the real process rather than a
# daemonized orphan. Same reason as every other longrun here.
#
# No notification-fd: nothing observes this service's readiness, and
# unlike `network` it records no state for convergence to compare
# against. It is up when acpid has its event files open.
# ============================================================
exec acpid -f
